CAT = "cpaaudPlanning"; SEG = "PLN"
Q = [
("The overall audit strategy for a nonissuer sets:",
 "The scope, timing and direction", ["The sample items to test", "The wording of the opinion", "Management's reporting deadline"],
 "The strategy sets the scope, timing and direction of the audit and guides the detailed audit plan, which lists the nature, timing and extent of procedures."),
("Analytical procedures used in planning an audit are meant mainly to:",
 "Identify unusual relationships", ["Replace tests of details", "Test the operating controls", "Confirm balances externally"],
 "Planning analytics help the auditor understand the entity and spot unexpected changes that may point to risks of material misstatement."),
("Under SAS 145, which is an inherent risk factor?",
 "Subjectivity of the measurement", ["The size of the audit fee", "The experience of the staff", "The firm's sampling method"],
 "SAS 145 lists complexity, subjectivity, change, uncertainty and susceptibility to misstatement from management bias or fraud as inherent risk factors."),
("Under SAS 145, a significant risk is one whose assessed inherent risk is:",
 "At the upper end of the spectrum", ["Below performance materiality", "Reduced by strong internal controls", "Found only in revenue accounts"],
 "SAS 145 assesses inherent risk on a spectrum. A significant risk is one at or near the upper end, or one that other standards require to be treated as significant."),
("Under SAS 145, when the auditor does not plan to test the operating effectiveness of controls, control risk is assessed:",
 "At the maximum", ["At the minimum", "At a moderate level", "At the inherent risk level"],
 "If the auditor does not plan to rely on controls, control risk is assessed at the maximum so that the risk of material misstatement equals inherent risk."),
("Using the audit risk model, if the assessed risk of material misstatement rises, the acceptable detection risk:",
 "Falls", ["Rises", "Stays the same", "Rises then falls"],
 "Audit risk equals the risk of material misstatement times detection risk. To hold audit risk at the planned level, detection risk must fall, so substantive procedures increase."),
("To lower detection risk for an account, an auditor would most likely:",
 "Test more items at year-end", ["Test controls at an interim date", "Raise performance materiality", "Use more inquiry alone"],
 "Lower detection risk calls for more persuasive substantive evidence: larger samples, more reliable procedures, and testing nearer the period end."),
("Performance materiality is set below overall materiality in order to:",
 "Allow for undetected misstatements", ["Increase the audit fee charged", "Cover only individually material errors", "Measure deviations from controls"],
 "Performance materiality reduces to an appropriately low level the probability that uncorrected and undetected misstatements together exceed materiality for the statements as a whole."),
("Late in the audit, the auditor learns that actual results differ sharply from the figures materiality was based on. The auditor should:",
 "Revise materiality", ["Keep the original amount", "Disclaim an opinion", "Restart the audit"],
 "Materiality is revisited when the auditor becomes aware of information that would have led to a different amount initially, and procedures are reconsidered."),
("Misstatements below the clearly trivial threshold:",
 "Need not be accumulated", ["Must be booked by the client", "Are reported to the SEC", "Lower materiality"],
 "The auditor accumulates misstatements identified during the audit other than those that are clearly trivial, meaning inconsequential individually and in total."),
("A benchmark commonly used to set materiality for a stable, profitable company is:",
 "Pretax income from operations", ["Total cash on hand at year-end", "The number of full-time employees", "The prior year's audit fee"],
 "Profit-oriented entities often use pretax income from continuing operations. Revenue or total assets suit entities with volatile or low earnings."),
("Which factor in understanding the entity's environment is most likely to raise inherent risk for inventory?",
 "Rapid technological change", ["Stable customer demand", "Long-serving warehouse staff", "A single product line"],
 "Fast-changing technology can make inventory obsolete, which raises the risk that the valuation assertion is misstated."),
("A falling economy with tighter credit is most likely to raise the risk of misstatement in:",
 "The allowance for credit losses", ["Prepaid insurance balances", "Common stock and paid-in capital", "Accrued payroll at year-end"],
 "In a downturn customers pay more slowly and default more often, raising the risk that receivables are overstated through too small an allowance."),
("When rising demand meets a fixed supply of a product, standard economics predicts:",
 "Higher prices", ["Lower prices", "Unchanged prices", "Lower quantities sold"],
 "With supply fixed, an increase in demand pushes the equilibrium price up. The auditor uses such economic factors to set expectations, for example for revenue."),
("Under SAS 145, the auditor's risk assessment procedures must include inquiries, observation and inspection, and:",
 "Analytical procedures", ["External confirmations", "Recalculation of balances", "Tests of details of balances"],
 "Risk assessment procedures comprise inquiries of management and others, analytical procedures, and observation and inspection."),
("The engagement team discussion required at planning is meant to consider:",
 "How the statements could be misstated", ["How to reduce the audit fee", "Which staff members to promote", "The client's tax planning for next year"],
 "The discussion considers how and where the statements might be susceptible to material misstatement, including due to fraud, and shares knowledge across the team."),
("Under SAS 145, the auditor must design and perform substantive procedures for:",
 "Each material account and disclosure", ["Only the significant risks identified", "Only accounts with known errors", "Only revenue and cash accounts"],
 "Regardless of the assessed risk, the auditor designs and performs substantive procedures for each relevant assertion of each material class of transactions, account balance and disclosure."),
("An auditor of a nonissuer uses a lower materiality for related party transactions because users focus on them. This is called:",
 "Materiality for particular items", ["Performance materiality", "The clearly trivial threshold", "Tolerable deviation rate"],
 "When lower misstatements in particular classes or disclosures could influence users, the auditor sets materiality levels for those particular items."),
("Assessing the risk of material misstatement at the assertion level combines inherent risk and:",
 "Control risk", ["Detection risk", "Sampling risk", "Business risk"],
 "The risk of material misstatement is the product of inherent risk and control risk. Detection risk is what the auditor controls through substantive work."),
("An audit of a first-year client of a nonissuer requires extra procedures on:",
 "Opening balances", ["Next year's budget", "The predecessor's fees", "Interim reviews"],
 "In an initial audit, the auditor obtains evidence that opening balances do not contain misstatements that materially affect the current statements and that policies were applied consistently."),
]
